---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';
import { TIP_LINE } from '../../data/site';

// Learn article 1 (s91). Every statement about ChatGPT or Claude is from their
// own help pages, read on 2026-10-06 and linked where it is made:
// - OpenAI, "Memory in ChatGPT" (help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148), updated
//   about 2026-09-19: Settings, Personalization, Memory; saved memories and
//   reference chat history; no import from other apps described.
// - Anthropic, "Import and export your memory from Claude"
//   (support.claude.com/en/articles/12123587), updated 2026-09-02: Free, Pro,
//   Max and Team, web and Claude Desktop; Settings, Memory, Start import; "Add
//   to memory"; experimental; up to 24 hours; work-related topics.
// - Anthropic, "Use Claude's chat search and memory"
//   (support.claude.com/en/articles/11817273): Settings, Memory; each project
//   has its own memory space.
// The chart is the session 64 live test (notes HANDOFF_V0.2_PART6_ARCHIVE "Session 64 live test"): 8 cross-app questions, 4 answered unprompted, 3 once told to check Zaaheen, 1 blocked by a Google add-on in Antigravity.
// Zaaheen's own steps are the connect guides' (/docs/connect-chatgpt/,
// /docs/connect-claude/), which are the app's own words.
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'why', title: "Why they don't share" },
  { id: 'copy', title: 'Copy and paste' },
  { id: 'import', title: "Claude's memory import" },
  { id: 'shared', title: 'One memory for both' },
  { id: 'choose', title: 'Which to choose' },
];
const OPENAI_MEMORY = 'https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8590148-memory-in-chatgpt';
const CLAUDE_IMPORT = 'https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12123587-import-and-export-your-memory-from-claude';
const CLAUDE_MEMORY = 'https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11817273-use-claude-s-chat-search-and-memory-to-build-on-previous-context';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/share-memory-between-chatgpt-and-claude/"
  lede="ChatGPT and Claude each keep their own memory, and neither can read the other's. You can copy what ChatGPT knows into Claude by hand, use Claude's memory import once, or connect both apps to one shared memory so a fact you tell either app is there in both."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why ChatGPT and Claude don't share memory</h2>
    <p>Both apps can remember you. ChatGPT keeps saved memories and can refer back to your past
       chats; you manage them under Settings, Personalization, Memory
       (<a href={OPENAI_MEMORY}>OpenAI's help page</a>). Claude builds a memory from your chats,
       with a separate memory for each project; you manage it under Settings, Memory
       (<a href={CLAUDE_MEMORY}>Anthropic's help page</a>).</p>
    <p>Each memory lives in that company's account, on that company's servers, and there is no
       link between the two. So the preferences ChatGPT learned over months mean nothing to
       Claude on the first day, and the reverse. People describe it the same way again and again:
       re-explaining who they are, what they're working on and how they like answers, every time
       they switch.</p>
    <p>There are three ways around it. They differ in effort and in one thing that matters a
       lot: whether they keep working after the first day.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="copy">
    <h2>Option 1: copy and paste</h2>
    <p>The simplest way costs nothing:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In ChatGPT, ask: "List everything you remember about me, as short bullet points."</li>
      <li>Read the list and remove anything you don't want to carry over.</li>
      <li>In Claude, start a chat, paste the list and say "Please remember these about me."</li>
    </ol>
    <p>It works for a handful of facts. The catch is that it is a snapshot. Anything you tell
       ChatGPT tomorrow stays in ChatGPT until you copy again, and the two memories drift apart
       from there.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="import">
    <h2>Option 2: Claude's memory import</h2>
    <p>Claude has a tool built for this move (<a href={CLAUDE_IMPORT}>Anthropic's help page</a>).
       It is on the Free, Pro, Max and Team plans, on the web and in Claude Desktop:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In Claude, open Settings, then Memory, and choose Start import. Claude gives you a
          prompt to copy.</li>
      <li>Paste that prompt into ChatGPT. It writes out what it remembers about you.</li>
      <li>Copy ChatGPT's answer back into Claude's box and choose Add to memory.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>It is quick, but Anthropic is clear about the limits: the import is experimental, it can
       take up to 24 hours to show up, and Claude keeps mainly work-related details, so some
       personal ones may not carry over. It also only goes one way, once. OpenAI's memory help
       page describes no import from other apps, and a fact you tell ChatGPT after the import
       never reaches Claude.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="shared">
    <h2>Option 3: one memory both apps use</h2>
    <p>The third way stops moving memories around. Instead of two memories, one inside each app,
       you keep one memory outside both, and connect each app to it. Whatever you tell ChatGPT is
       saved there, and Claude reads it from there, today and next month.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-chatgpt-claude"
      from="ChatGPT"
      to="Claude"
      fact="Prefers short answers"
      caption="You tell ChatGPT a preference once. It is saved to the one memory on your computer, and Claude recalls it from there, in any later chat."
    />

    <p>Apps connect to tools like this through MCP, the standard way AI apps plug into tools and
       data. Zaaheen is one such memory: an app for Windows and Mac that keeps your memories
       encrypted on your own computer, where ChatGPT, Claude and your other connected AI apps can
       save to it and look things up. Setting it up for these two apps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Claude Desktop:</strong> Zaaheen's Agents tab saves an extension you install in
          Claude (<a href="/docs/connect-claude/">step by step</a>). It works in Claude's Chat
          and Cowork.</li>
      <li><strong>ChatGPT:</strong> the ChatGPT app for your computer, where you add Zaaheen under
          Settings, Plugins, MCP, and use it in a Work or Codex chat
          (<a href="/docs/connect-chatgpt/">step by step</a>). ChatGPT's Chat mode, and ChatGPT in
          a web browser, can't connect to apps on your computer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="learn-note">Both apps keep using their own memory too, and won't always think of a
       shared one by themselves. Add this line to each app's instructions so they do:
       "{TIP_LINE}"</p>
    <p>To bring over what ChatGPT already knows, ask ChatGPT once, after connecting: "Save
       everything you remember about me to Zaaheen." From then on there is nothing left to copy.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="choose">
    <h2>Which option to choose</h2>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Effort</th><th scope="col">After the first day</th><th scope="col">Where it's kept</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Copy and paste</th><td>A few minutes, every time</td><td>Drifts apart until you copy again</td><td>In each app's account</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Claude's memory import</th><td>A few minutes, once</td><td>Only ChatGPT to Claude, only what was there at the time</td><td>In Claude's account</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">One shared memory</th><td>Connect each app once</td><td>Both apps save and recall, both ways</td><td>Wherever that memory runs; with Zaaheen, on your computer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>We tested a shared memory ourselves. Five AI apps on one Windows computer were connected
       to the same Zaaheen memory, and we asked eight questions about the person, such as their
       favourite tea, their favourite city and when they like meetings, in a different app from
       the one that saved the answer.</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-shared-recall"
      title="8 questions about the person, asked across 5 AI apps sharing one memory"
      measure="Questions"
      max={8}
      bars={[
        { label: 'Answered by itself', value: 4, note: 'of 8' },
        { label: 'Once told to check', value: 3, note: 'of 8' },
        { label: 'Blocked by an add-on', value: 1, note: 'of 8' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: "Zaaheen's own live test, 26 September 2026, Windows, with Claude Desktop, Cursor, ChatGPT (Work and Codex) and Antigravity. The blocked answer was a Google add-on crashing Antigravity's tool calls before they reached Zaaheen." }}
    />

    <p>Every app that reached the memory gave the right answer. Half the time the app checked by
       itself; the other times it answered from its own memory first and needed "check Zaaheen".
       That is why the line above, in each app's instructions, matters.</p>

    <p>If you only use the two apps now and then, copying a short list once may be all you need.
       If you're moving from ChatGPT to Claude for good, Claude's import is made for that. If you
       use both, every week, a shared memory is the one that doesn't go stale.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
